From: Pedro Baltazar <pedrobtz@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH P11] gguf : validate a key's type before it is a gguf_type

Upstream pull request: (not opened yet; see design §9.3)

gguf_init_from_reader() read a key-value pair's type, and an array's
element type, straight into an enum gguf_type and compared it with
GGUF_TYPE_ARRAY before the switch rejected unknown types. A malformed
file whose type is outside the enumeration loads an invalid enum value
first, which is undefined in C++ (UBSan: "load of value ..., which is
not a valid value for type 'gguf_type'"); so does a failed read, which
leaves the variable at its initial gguf_type(-1). Found by zulm's fuzz
target for files. The reader now refuses such a type, so the read fails
as for a truncated file, and the variable starts at GGUF_TYPE_COUNT.

--- a/src/gguf.cpp
+++ b/src/gguf.cpp
@@ -336,6 +336,11 @@
         if (!read(tmp)) {
             return false;
         }
+        // a value outside the enumeration must not be loaded into a gguf_type
+        if (tmp < 0 || tmp >= GGUF_TYPE_COUNT) {
+            GGML_LOG_ERROR("%s: invalid gguf type %d\n", __func__, (int) tmp);
+            return false;
+        }
         dst = gguf_type(tmp);
         return true;
     }
@@ -555,7 +560,9 @@
 
         for (int64_t i = 0; ok && i < n_kv; ++i) {
             std::string key;
-            gguf_type   type     = gguf_type(-1);
+            // a valid enumerator: -1 is outside the enumeration, and the
+            // comparison below reads it when the read fails
+            gguf_type   type     = GGUF_TYPE_COUNT;
             bool        is_array = false;
             uint64_t    n        = 1;
 
